{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Go now, see, etc.</b> Now he explains the mission of the mitzvah that he is performing, and he said <b>and bring me back a word</b> — he made him an emissary to bring back a response to him. By this, even according to the one who says (Pesachim 8b) that those sent on a mitzvah are not harmed — that applies specifically on their going but not on their return — in this case he made him an emissary even for his return as well. From then on, he is assured that he will return in peace to his father." "<b>And furthermore,</b> even though his brothers hated him and the danger was frequent (shekhich hezeika), Jacob holds according to the opinion of the Sages who disagree with Pelimo in Pesachim (8b), and this is its language: It was taught in a baraita: One inspects the crevice between [the lands of] Israel and Aram up to the place where his hand reaches. Pelimo says: his entire body is not inspected because of the danger. And they object: but did not Rabbi Elazar say that those sent on a mitzvah are not harmed? And they answer: where the danger is frequent, it is different, etc., until here. And the Sages who disagree with Pelimo hold that one can distinguish between the case before us and the case of Samuel, for there the danger was certainly frequent (shekhich hezeika), for Saul would hear that David is being anointed as king in his place, and there is no shekhich hezeika like that — whereas the concern lest a gentile plot against him is not called shekhich hezeika. From this we may infer regarding our case that the brothers were not definitively shekhich hezeika, for it would not enter Jacob's mind that they would hate him so intensely that the mission of a mitzvah would not protect him from their hand. Also that case of the shaky ladder (sulam ra'ua) that they said in Kiddushin (39b) — shekhich hezeika is greater than the inspection of a crevice between a Jew and a gentile — and in the case of the brothers, who are not so suspected of doing evil to their brother. And perhaps even according to Pelimo's reasoning one may say that it is not considered shekhich hezeika in this case of Jacob's sons, and the mission of a mitzvah protects from harm." "<b>And if</b> you say: if so, why did what happened to Joseph happen — that he was sold as a slave? It may be answered that harm whose ultimate purpose is benefit and great elevation is not considered harm." "<b>Still another</b> reason there is in the matter, and it is that Jacob, peace be upon him, was precise in his words regarding the mission: <b>Are not your brothers tending the flock in Shechem? Come, and I will send you to them.</b> Behold, he explicitly revealed his intention that his mission was to Shechem. And when Joseph went and did not find them in Shechem and went to search for them in another place, behold, he is his own emissary (sheliach atzmo), for his father did not send him to go to another place, and there is no mitzvah-mission present here — therefore what happened, happened. And Jacob intended, by specifying for him the place, that he foresaw through the holy spirit (ruach ha-kodesh) that to another place he would call him disaster (ason), as indeed happened, whereas on the road to Shechem no evil would befall him. And Joseph, peace be upon him, thought that when his father told him Shechem, he was merely indicating a place for him — and the matter was not precise. And HaShem brought about reversal after reversal (gilgel gilgalim) to do what He had purposed to do." "<b>And he came to Shechem.</b> The place to which his father sent him, and he did not find them, and he was searching for them. And there was no need to state this, for it is understood from his saying ״וַיִּמְצָאֵהוּ אִישׁ וְהִנֵּה תֹעֶה״ after he came to Shechem."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {1 "The Talmudic case attributed here to Samuel refers to his anointing of David despite the danger from Saul (see Pesachim 8b).", 3 "The phrase גִּלְגֵּל גִּלְגּוּלִים is rendered literally; the repeated Hebrew root ג-ל-ל carries the wordplay."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:43.684665570Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}